window.DOCS_INDEX=[{"p":"Quick start","h":"Quick start","u":"getting-started.html#quick-start","x":"Waypoints in your own scene in about five minutes. No code."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"1. Add the HUD","u":"getting-started.html#1-add-the-hud","x":"Open Tools > Bearing > Setup Wizard . Step 1 finds your camera and your player (tagged Player, or with a Character Controller). Pick a look (Explorer, Tactical, Minimal or Holo) and press Add HUD . The HUD shows in the Game view straight away, even before you press Play."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"2. Mark your objects","u":"getting-started.html#2-mark-your-objects","x":"Select the objects players should find (an objective, a shop, a door, a friend) in the Hierarchy or the Scene view. In step 2 of the wizard, pick an icon and press Add Waypoint . Each object gets a Waypoint named after it (\"SM_Old_Mill\" becomes \"Old Mill\"). You can also use Add Component > Bearing > Waypoint , or right-click in the Hierarchy and choose Bearing > Waypoint ."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"3. Pick the objective","u":"getting-started.html#3-pick-the-objective","x":"Select the most important waypoint and tick Current objective . It gets the objective colour, a gentle pulse, its name on screen, and it is shown first when the screen is crowded."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"4. Press Play","u":"getting-started.html#4-press-play","x":"Walk around. Objects on screen get a marker, objects off screen or behind you get an arrow at the edge with the distance, and everything shows on the compass and the radar."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"5. Optional: a map under the radar","u":"getting-started.html#5-optional-a-map-under-the-radar","x":"Step 3 of the wizard adds a Waypoint Map sized to your scene and takes a picture from above, so the radar works as a minimap. See Map under the radar ."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"Want to see everything working first?","u":"getting-started.html#want-to-see-everything-working-first","x":"Tools > Bearing > Create Example Map makes a small level in a new scene with every map feature in use: a place to discover, the objective with a beacon, a travel point locked behind it, a region uncovered by a lookout tower, a search area, the player's pins, search, and saving on F5 and F9. Press Play and walk (WASD, M for the map)."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"Something not showing?","u":"getting-started.html#something-not-showing","x":"Open Tools > Bearing > Why Can't I See It? . It checks the scene, fixes common problems in one click and explains every waypoint in plain words. While playing, press F8 for the same answers in the game (add the Waypoint Debug Overlay component to the HUD)."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Demo scenes","u":"samples.html#demo-scenes","x":"Open them from Tools > Bearing > Open Demo . Click the game to look around with the mouse; Escape frees the mouse. Tab switches the look, F8 opens the debug overlay, F1 hides the panel. Demo What it shows Showcase (follow the trail) An island with a short story: the expedition's canoe went missing, so cross the Old Bridge, climb to the Watch Stone, find the Lost Canoe and head back to camp (reach one and the next switches on). A trader, ruins, a lookout, hidden supplies and a scout around it; a route to the current objective that keeps to the trails (a Waypoint Route set to Prefer roads and tr"},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Using the demo scripts","u":"samples.html#using-the-demo-scripts","x":"The demo player controllers and the panel are for the demos only (Samples folder). Your game uses its own controller: waypoints need nothing from it."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Fit it into your game","u":"fit-your-game.html#fit-it-into-your-game","x":"How Bearing behaves in a real project, with your own controller, cameras, UI, quests and saves. Each case says what Bearing does by itself and what (if anything) you do."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Your player and camera","u":"fit-your-game.html#your-player-and-camera","x":"Your setup What happens What you do First person (camera inside the player) Distances from the player Nothing Third person (camera behind the player) Distances and reaching are measured from the object tagged Player , found by itself (again after a respawn) Tag your character Player, or set the HUD's Player No Player tag Distances come from the camera; the HUD inspector and the scene check say so Set Player for third person Cinemachine Cinemachine drives one real Camera (with the Cinemachine Brain); the HUD uses that camera Nothing (or set View Camera to the Brain's camera) Switching cameras ("},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Your UI","u":"fit-your-game.html#your-ui","x":"Your setup What happens What you do uGUI canvases, UI Toolkit menus The HUD is its own UI Toolkit panel. It never catches the mouse or gamepad focus: your buttons keep working through it Raise or lower Sorting Order to put it above or below your panels Your own health bar, quest list or ammo panel The compass and radar move with the style (top or bottom, any corner, margins) Add Keep clear of my own HUD areas on the HUD: edge arrows stay out of them. Orange boxes show them in the Game view Phones (notches, rounded corners) Everything stays inside the screen's safe area, re-read every frame Not"},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Your objects","u":"fit-your-game.html#your-objects","x":"Your setup What happens What you do Spawned enemies and pickups A prefab with a Waypoint shows up when it spawns and disappears when it's destroyed or switched off Nothing Pooled objects A reused object keeps \"reached\" until you reset it Call ResetReached() when you take it from the pool A spot with no object (a quest coordinate) Waypoint.CreateAt(position, \"Drop Zone\", WaypointIcon.Pin) ; move it with Position , delete with Remove() Moving targets (NPCs, vehicles) The marker follows every frame, above the object's top For a steady point, turn Mark Above Object off and use Offset Hundreds of w"},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Your quests and saves","u":"fit-your-game.html#your-quests-and-saves","x":"Your setup What happens What you do A quest system Waypoints only show where to go Call Show() , Hide() , SetAsCurrentObjective() , or use the On Reached event to tell your quest system Visual Scripting, Timeline, UnityEvents Every action above is a public method or property Call them from a graph, a Signal or a UnityEvent Saving WaypointSave.Capture() gives a short text for your save Store it; call WaypointSave.Restore(text) after loading. Waypoints in scenes loaded later pick up their state when they load Saving with no save system of your own SaveService.Save(\"slot1\") and SaveService.Load(\""},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Places from other products","u":"fit-your-game.html#places-from-other-products","x":"When another Rare Geometry product offers places (Snapbuild's built pieces, Interactra's doors), Bearing shows them on the compass, the minimap, the full-screen map and its legend with no setup, and follows them as they change. They are never saved by Bearing and never offered back. To choose what shows, add Waypoint External Places and give each source a rule (show or not, category, icon or the source's own picture, colour, and which displays). Whether players may track or fast travel to them is in Player Map Permissions (Other Products). The Chart Table's Places tab lists them under From oth"},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Split screen","u":"fit-your-game.html#split-screen","x":"GameObject > Bearing > Split Screen (second player) gives the first camera the top half, makes a second camera for the bottom half and a second HUD with the same look. Move the new camera onto your second player and set the new HUD's Player. Each HUD keeps its own map, floor, marks and sightings."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Quick saving","u":"fit-your-game.html#quick-saving","x":"Waypoint Save Keys saves everything Bearing knows to a slot on F5 and loads it on F9 (both keys and the slot are settings), with a banner saying so. Your own save system can call SaveService or WaypointSave.Capture and Restore instead."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Not included","u":"fit-your-game.html#not-included","x":"A live camera minimap, fog of war, routes along paths, 3D beacons in the world, networking (each player's HUD is local) and VR. Arrows and distances are straight lines, not paths."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"Common tasks and recipes","u":"common-tasks.html#common-tasks-and-recipes","x":"Short answers to \"how do I...\". Each points to the page with the details."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"Show the player where to go next","u":"common-tasks.html#show-the-player-where-to-go-next","x":"Add a Waypoint to the target (Add Component > Bearing > Waypoint) and set its State to Active . Players see a marker on screen, an arrow at the edge with the distance, an icon on the compass and the minimap, and a pin on the full-screen map. Explorer also lights a beacon over it. When it's done, set the State to Completed (or use When Reached: Done) and make the next place Active. A Waypoint Chain does this for you (Chart Table, Chains tab). See Chart Table ."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"Hide places until they are found","u":"common-tasks.html#hide-places-until-they-are-found","x":"Set the place's State to Undiscovered and Discover to When Near or When Seen . Undiscovered places show as \"?\" (the style's States section). For \"somewhere around here\", give it a Search area (Discovery and fast travel). See Fog of war and region discovery ."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"A lookout tower that uncovers the map","u":"common-tasks.html#a-lookout-tower-that-uncovers-the-map","x":"GameObject > Bearing > Region Reveal > Tower , move it onto your tower and set its radius. Or, on a Map Region, pick Revealed by: A lookout tower . See Towers, maps and story reveals ."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"Fast travel with a lock and a cost","u":"common-tasks.html#fast-travel-with-a-lock-and-a-cost","x":"On the place, press Make it a travel point (Discovery and fast travel). Set When Place Completed and the place it needs, and a Locked Reason. Costs: WaypointTravelPoint.Check = p => gold < 50 ? \"Costs 50 gold\" : null; . See Players on the map ."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"A building with floors","u":"common-tasks.html#a-building-with-floors","x":"Give the building its own Waypoint Map with Is Area on and its floors (heights); press Bake All Floors . Players switch map automatically when they walk in. See Maps ."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"Change the whole look","u":"common-tasks.html#change-the-whole-look","x":"Pick a look on the HUD (Explorer, Tactical, Minimal, Holo), then Save as my own style and change anything. Fine-tune positions and sizes in the style's Layout section. See Make it yours ."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"Let players choose their own settings","u":"common-tasks.html#let-players-choose-their-own-settings","x":"Players press O (or the map's Settings button). Choose what they may change and the slider limits with a Player Options asset (Chart Table gear, The player's options). From your own menu: WaypointPlayerSettings.Current then WaypointPlayerSettings.Apply() . See Players on the map ."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"The mouse cursor while playing","u":"common-tasks.html#the-mouse-cursor-while-playing","x":"Bearing frees and shows the cursor whenever something the player can click is up: the full-screen map and the player options. Players can also hold Alt at any time for a free cursor (to click the HUD's buttons) and let go to look around again; a small hint (\"Esc or hold Alt for the cursor\") shows for a few seconds each time the mouse is captured. In a browser build, if the page loses or never gets pointer lock (Esc, a refused request), the cursor comes back instead of staying hidden. On the Waypoint HUD : Cursor Policy : Free For Bearing Ui (the default, as above) or Game Owns It (Bearing leav"},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"Show it in another language","u":"common-tasks.html#show-it-in-another-language","x":"Create a Text Table , press Collect Words , fill it in, set it on the HUD (More, Language). With Unity Localization add Waypoint Localization . See Code and your own UI ."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"Use my own HUD art or my own UI","u":"common-tasks.html#use-my-own-hud-art-or-my-own-ui","x":"Your own icons: Icon = Custom and your sprite (Keep its own colours for full-colour pictures). Your own marker background and arrow: the style's Marker and Edge Arrows sections. Your own UI for one part: turn Bearing's part off and add an IWaypointDisplay (see Code and your own UI )."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"Save and load","u":"common-tasks.html#save-and-load","x":"SaveService.Save(\"slot\") and SaveService.Load(\"slot\") , or WaypointSave.Capture() and Restore(json) with your own save system. To try it: add Waypoint Save Keys (F5 and F9). See Trails, events and saving ."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"Split screen","u":"common-tasks.html#split-screen","x":"GameObject > Bearing > Split Screen (second player) . See Fit it into your game ."},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"Recipes from code","u":"common-tasks.html#recipes-from-code","x":"// Mark a quest target when a quest starts, and clear it when the quest ends. var w = target.AddComponent<Waypoint>(); w.Label = \"Find the smith\"; w.Icon = WaypointIcon.Talk; w.State = PoiState.Active; // ... later w.State = PoiState.Completed; // Open the map at a place (for a \"show on map\" button in your quest log). hud.OpenMap(); hud.SearchMap(\"smith\"); hud.PickSearchResult(); // Tell the player something in the HUD's own banner. hud.ShowBanner(\"Bridge repaired\", \"Chapter 2\"); // React to discoveries (an achievement, a sound of your own). WaypointRegistry.AnyStateChanged += (place, from, to"},{"p":"Common tasks and recipes","h":"A heartbeat near the objective","u":"common-tasks.html#a-heartbeat-near-the-objective","x":"Add Waypoint Sounds (on the HUD) and set Heartbeat to a short beat. Inside Heartbeat Range (40 m by default) of the tracked place, or else the current objective, it beats, quicker the closer the player gets (Heartbeat Interval: seconds between beats at the edge and right at the place). sounds.HeartbeatGap gives the gap now for your own effects."},{"p":"Chart Table: author and test your places","h":"Chart Table: author and test your places","u":"chart-table.html#chart-table-author-and-test-your-places","x":"Tools > Bearing > Chart Table is where you author every place in your level and test it while the HUD runs. Every waypoint is a place with a state, and one state change updates every display in the same frame: the marker on screen, the edge arrow, the compass bar, the minimap, the full-screen map and its legend. Try it first in the demo: Tools > Bearing > Open Demo > Chart Table ."},{"p":"Chart Table: author and test your places","h":"Place states","u":"chart-table.html#place-states","x":"State What players see (the defaults) Undiscovered Not found yet: a faint question mark on the compass, minimap and map; no screen marker or edge arrow; no name. Discovered Found: shown normally everywhere. Active The current objective: the objective colour and a pulse, everywhere, shown first when the screen is crowded. Completed Done: off the screen and compass, dimmed with a check mark on the minimap and map. Hidden Drawn nowhere, but still listed in the Chart Table. Each state's look is a setting in the HUD's style ( States section; also behind the Chart Table's gear): which displays show "},{"p":"Chart Table: author and test your places","h":"The tabs","u":"chart-table.html#the-tabs","x":"Tab What it does Places Every place in the open scenes, sorted by floor and name. Search by name, category or object; filter by state and category. Edit the name, icon, state, category and floor right in the list. Click a row to select and frame it. Make places from selection turns every selected object into a place in one step (one Undo). Test Step the picked place through its states (or press Next state ) and watch Every display, live : for each display it says Shown or why not. Works in Play Mode and, with the HUD's editor preview on, while you edit. Chains An objective chain is places in o"},{"p":"Chart Table: author and test your places","h":"Discovery","u":"chart-table.html#discovery","x":"An undiscovered place becomes discovered by one of (Waypoint > Discovery and fast travel): When the player is near (Discover Within, in metres), the default; When the player can see it : on screen, within the distance, and not behind a wall; When its region is discovered (pick the Waypoint Map Region); Only when told : from code, a UnityEvent or the Chart Table. Reaching a place always discovers it. On discovery the discovery banner shows \"New location discovered\" and the name. Set it in the style's Banner section: words and name, name only, icon only, or none; top, upper third, centre or bott"},{"p":"Chart Table: author and test your places","h":"Fast travel","u":"chart-table.html#fast-travel","x":"Tick Fast-travel point on a place. Once discovered it carries a small arrow badge on the full-screen map; clicking it (or the gamepad's place button on it) raises On Fast Travel Requested on the place, Waypoint.FastTravelRequested , WaypointHud.FastTravelRequested and WaypointRegistry.AnyFastTravelRequested . Your game does the move (and any fade, cost or scene load). The demo's SampleFastTravel shows the simplest version."},{"p":"Chart Table: author and test your places","h":"Saving","u":"chart-table.html#saving","x":"Place states are saved with everything else Bearing saves ( bearing.waypoints , format version 2): with SaveService.Save(\"slot\") (one JSON file per slot, or your save product when one is installed) or your own save system through WaypointSave.Capture() and Restore(text) . Each place is found again by its Stable Id."},{"p":"Chart Table: author and test your places","h":"From code","u":"chart-table.html#from-code","x":"using RareGeometry.Bearing; using RareGeometry.Core; place.State = PoiState.Active; // every display updates place.StateChanged += (w, from, to) => { }; // or WaypointRegistry.AnyStateChanged place.Discover(); // only if undiscovered chain.Begin(); // a WaypointChain: first step Active Other products read Bearing's places through the shared IPoiSource ( WaypointPoiSource ): id, name, position, state, category and icon."},{"p":"Chart Table: author and test your places","h":"As the player","u":"chart-table.html#as-the-player","x":"In Play Mode the Test tab's As the player row does what a player would do with the picked place: show it on the map, track it, travel there (the permissions, the lock and your checks all apply), pin it, search for it and discover it. The line under it says whether travel is open and why not, and what the last refused action was."},{"p":"Chart Table: author and test your places","h":"Narrow windows","u":"chart-table.html#narrow-windows","x":"The Places list fits any width. The name always gets the spare room; as the window narrows, Floor hides first, then Category, then the icon colour and Icon columns. Point at a row to see what the hidden columns hold (and the full name). The headings sit exactly over their columns. Below about 470 points the search and the two filters stack, each full width. The tabs go from one row to an even grid (3 and 2, in the same columns), then to icons only, then to a dropdown. Category is picked, not typed: the dropdown lists the categories already used, None, and New category... . Colour is the icon c"},{"p":"Routes, tracker, floors and map filters","h":"Routes, tracker, floors and map filters","u":"routes.html#routes-tracker-floors-and-map-filters","x":""},{"p":"Routes, tracker, floors and map filters","h":"Routes","u":"routes.html#routes","x":"Add a Waypoint Route (GameObject > Bearing, or Add Component > Bearing > Waypoint Route; the HUD's object is a good place). With no Target it leads to the current objective (the nearest Active place); give it a Target, or let the player track a place, to lead somewhere else. It works like a game's GPS: the route is worked out once and then kept steady. The line shortens as the player follows it, and it is only worked out again when the player strays more than Stray Distance from it, the place moves, or a clearly shorter way opens up (checked every few seconds, taken only when 15% shorter). It "},{"p":"Routes, tracker, floors and map filters","h":"Modes","u":"routes.html#modes","x":"Mode What it does Fastest (NavMesh) The quickest walking path round walls and water, from a baked NavMesh (Window > AI > Navigation, or NavMesh Surface). Prefer roads and trails Along your roads and trails when that is at most Road Detour times the direct way (1.35 = up to 35% longer), the fastest way on and off them. Otherwise the direct fastest way. Roads and trails only Along the roads wherever they go (when the player is within Road Reach of one), the fastest way only to get on and off. Straight line A straight line to the place (flying, sailing, open plains). My points Your own points in "},{"p":"Routes, tracker, floors and map filters","h":"When there is no path, and when the line shows","u":"routes.html#when-there-is-no-path-and-when-the-line-shows","x":"Fallback is what shows when there is no path (no NavMesh, the place off it, no road near): Straight line , Compass only (no line, the compass cue still shows the way) or Hide . Status says what is shown (NavMesh, NavMeshPartial, Roads, Manual, StraightLine, Fallback) and Reason says why in plain words; Tools > Bearing > Diagnostics lists it. Show When: Always ; Tracked or set places only (no line to the automatic nearest objective, only to a place the player tracks or your game sets as the Target); Map open only (the world and minimap stay clear); A few seconds after setting (the line shows fo"},{"p":"Routes, tracker, floors and map filters","h":"Objective tracker","u":"routes.html#objective-tracker","x":"A short list in a screen corner: every Active place (the current objectives, nearest first), then up to Max Tracked places you set Tracked on (Waypoint > Discovery and fast travel > Show in the tracker), each with its icon, name and distance. Style section Tracker : on/off, corner, margins, how many, distances, icon size, title, panel colour. Undiscovered places show as \"?\"."},{"p":"Routes, tracker, floors and map filters","h":"Compass bearing readout and height arrows","u":"routes.html#compass-bearing-readout-and-height-arrows","x":"Compass > Show Bearing writes the heading under the centre mark: NE 45° or 045° (Bearing As). The direction letters come from the compass's direction words, so they follow your language. Marker > Show Height Arrow : a small up or down arrow beside a marker (and its edge arrow) when the place is more than Height Threshold metres above or below the player. The compass and minimap have their own height arrows."},{"p":"Routes, tracker, floors and map filters","h":"Floors","u":"routes.html#floors","x":"For buildings and levels mapped one floor at a time, fill the Waypoint Map's Floors list: a name, the height range (world Y; Z in 2D) and the floor's own picture. The minimap shows the player's floor. The full-screen map opens on the player's floor; the up and down buttons at its lower right step through the floors, with the floor name above them. Places carry a Floor number (0 = the first floor in the list); places on other floors are dimmed."},{"p":"Routes, tracker, floors and map filters","h":"Map legend and filters","u":"routes.html#map-legend-and-filters","x":"The full-screen map's places legend (top right) lists the place states that are on the map (Places, Objective, Done...) and their categories. Click a row to hide or show those places. From code: WaypointMapFilters.SetStateShown , SetCategoryShown , ShowAll . Filters are saved with the rest."},{"p":"Routes, tracker, floors and map filters","h":"Saving","u":"routes.html#saving","x":"WaypointSave (format 3) and the shared save also keep: which places are tracked, the map filters and the route's target."},{"p":"Players on the map: pins, stamps, tracking and fast travel","h":"Players on the map: pins, stamps, tracking and fast travel","u":"player-map.html#players-on-the-map-pins-stamps-tracking-and-fast-travel","x":"Players can mark the map, track places and fast travel, as far as you allow. You decide with one asset."},{"p":"Players on the map: pins, stamps, tracking and fast travel","h":"Player Map Permissions","u":"player-map.html#player-map-permissions","x":"Create > Bearing > Player Map Permissions (or Chart Table > gear > What players may do on the map ), then give it to the Waypoint HUD ( What players may do ). With none, the Open World preset applies. Split screen: each player's HUD can have its own. Preset Players may Guided Story Look, filter and track. No marks of their own, no fast travel. Open World (default) Place pins (5) and stamps (100), choose their icon, colour and name, move and delete them, track, filter, fast travel to discovered travel points after a confirm. Survival Cartographer Everything, with notes, 10 pins and 300 stamps. "},{"p":"Players on the map: pins, stamps, tracking and fast travel","h":"Pins and stamps","u":"player-map.html#pins-and-stamps","x":"Pins are few and show everywhere (screen, compass, minimap, map): for getting somewhere. Stamps are many and show on the map only: for remembering things. On the full-screen map, click an empty spot and pick Pin or Stamp . Click a mark to open its card: icon, colour, name, note, Move (the next click puts it there) and Delete , each only when allowed. At the limit, a new mark replaces the oldest of its kind. Marks are listed in the legend as \"My pins\" and \"My stamps\", so they can be hidden. From code: PlayerMarks.Add , AddOnMap , SetIcon , SetColour , Rename , SetNote , Move , Remove , Clear , "},{"p":"Players on the map: pins, stamps, tracking and fast travel","h":"Tracking","u":"player-map.html#tracking","x":"The place card has Track for places players may track: the place joins the tracker and becomes the route's target (when the scene has a Waypoint Route). Tracking another place stops tracking the last. From code: hud.Track(place) , hud.Untrack() , hud.TrackedPlace , hud.CanTrack(place) ."},{"p":"Players on the map: pins, stamps, tracking and fast travel","h":"Fast travel","u":"player-map.html#fast-travel","x":"The place card shows Fast travel with the reason when it can't be used now: \"Discover it first\", \"Fast travel is off\", or your game's own reason. Your check: hud.FastTravelCheck = place => inCombat ? \"Not while fighting\" : null; (costs and cooldowns work the same way). With Confirm Travel on, the button asks \"Travel to Camp?\" first. Your game still does the move ( hud.FastTravelRequested , the place's On Fast Travel Requested). From code: hud.TravelStatus , hud.CanTravelTo , hud.TravelTo ."},{"p":"Players on the map: pins, stamps, tracking and fast travel","h":"Travel points that do the whole job","u":"player-map.html#travel-points-that-do-the-whole-job","x":"The place inspector's Make it a travel point adds a Waypoint Travel Point with an Arrival spot in front of the place. It then: opens when the place is discovered, when another place is completed (\"Light the lighthouse first.\"), or when your game calls OpenNow() ; shows its Locked Reason on the card until then; asks your cost or cooldown check: WaypointTravelPoint.Check = p => gold < 50 ? \"Costs 50 gold\" : null; moves the player to the Arrival (Character Controllers and Rigidbodies handled) and closes the map. Turn Move Player off to do the move yourself (a scene load, a fade) in On Travel ."},{"p":"Players on the map: pins, stamps, tracking and fast travel","h":"Reduced motion","u":"player-map.html#reduced-motion","x":"WaypointAccessibility.ReducedMotion = true; makes every reveal animation an instant change and stops pulsing. Put it in your options menu."},{"p":"Players on the map: pins, stamps, tracking and fast travel","h":"Saving","u":"player-map.html#saving","x":"WaypointSave (format 4) and the shared save keep the player's pins and stamps (with icon, colour, name, note and tracking) and Reduced Motion."},{"p":"World scan and moving entities","h":"World scan and moving entities","u":"scan-and-entities.html#world-scan-and-moving-entities","x":""},{"p":"World scan and moving entities","h":"World scan","u":"scan-and-entities.html#world-scan","x":"Add a Waypoint World Scan (GameObject > Bearing > World Scan puts it on the HUD's player). A scan sends a pulse out from the player: places it reaches are found as the pulse passes them. Undiscovered places are discovered (and play their reveal animation); places already known play a highlight. Setting What it does Radius, Speed How far the pulse goes (metres) and how fast it spreads (metres per second). Cooldown Seconds before it can scan again. Key A key that scans (None = only from your code or a UnityEvent). Line Of Sight Only places the scan can see (Blocked By: the layers that block it)."},{"p":"World scan and moving entities","h":"Moving entities","u":"scan-and-entities.html#moving-entities","x":"Add a Waypoint Entity to anything that moves: enemies, allies, vehicles, mounts, NPCs, other players (GameObject > Bearing > Moving Entity, with the object selected). It adds a Waypoint shown on the minimap and map only. Setting What it does Faction Neutral, Ally, Enemy, Vehicle, Player or Custom: its colour comes from the style's Moving entities section. Show When Always; In Range (Show Range metres); In Line Of Sight (in range and nothing in the way); When Spotted (only after your code calls Spot() , for Spotted Seconds). Last Known Seconds When it is lost, a faded marker stays where it was "},{"p":"Sharing markers with other players","h":"Sharing markers with other players","u":"sharing.html#sharing-markers-with-other-players","x":"Players can share their pins and stamps, and ping a spot (\"look here\"), with the other players: co-op, split screen or online. Bearing does not tie you to a networking library. Add Waypoint Marker Sharing to an object in the scene (one per local player). Set the player's name and colour. Give it a transport: - Split screen / local co-op: sharing.Transport = new LocalMarkerTransport(\"my game\"); for each player (same channel name). - Netcode for GameObjects: add Netcode Marker Transport to the same object. It appears only when Netcode for GameObjects (2.0 or newer) is installed. The host passes "},{"p":"The Waypoint component","h":"The Waypoint component","u":"waypoint.html#the-waypoint-component","x":"Add it to anything players should find. Everything except the icon is optional."},{"p":"The Waypoint component","h":"Icon, name and colour","u":"waypoint.html#icon-name-and-colour","x":"Setting What it does Icon Pick from 20 built-in icons (objective, pin, flag, star, home, skull, chest, key, coin, talk...), or Custom for your own sprite. White sprites take the colour best. Name The words players see, for example \"Old Mill\". Leave empty for no name. State Undiscovered, Discovered, Active (the objective), Completed or Hidden; each state has its own look on every display (see Chart Table ). Current objective (Active) Objective colour, a gentle pulse, its name always on screen, and first place when the screen is crowded. Make this the only objective clears it on every other wayp"},{"p":"The Waypoint component","h":"Show on","u":"waypoint.html#show-on","x":"Each of Screen (marker) , Screen edge (arrow) , Compass , Radar , Full-screen map and Distance can be Style default (follow the HUD's look), Always or Never for this waypoint only. For example a hidden treasure can show on the radar but not on screen. The buttons under them set everything in one click: Button Shows Good for Everywhere marker, edge arrow, compass, radar, map objectives and places Radar and map radar blip and map pin only enemies, allies, vehicles (they follow their object as it moves) Map only map pin only shops, points of interest Screen only the marker when it is in view a ne"},{"p":"The Waypoint component","h":"Reaching it, distances and route","u":"waypoint.html#reaching-it-distances-and-route","x":"Distances are measured from the HUD's player (see Fit it into your game ). Setting What it does Reached within The player has reached it inside this distance (metres). 0 = never reached. When reached Nothing (just the events), Hide while the player is near, Done: switch this waypoint off, Done: mark it completed, or Done: show the next waypoint (this one becomes Completed; a trail; see Objective trails ). Next waypoint The waypoint switched on by \"show the next waypoint\". The objective moves along with it. Hide when closer than Hide the markers when the player is closer than this. 0 = off. Hid"},{"p":"The Waypoint component","h":"Placement, priority and group","u":"waypoint.html#placement-priority-and-group","x":"Setting What it does Mark Above Object The marker sits just above the object's top (from its renderers or colliders), not at its pivot. Offset Moves the marker, in metres. Priority When the screen is crowded, higher numbers are shown first. Group A name such as \"Shops\" so a whole group can be shown or hidden at once ( WaypointRegistry.SetGroupVisible ). Save Id Used by WaypointSave ; filled in for you. The scene check warns if two waypoints share one."},{"p":"The Waypoint component","h":"Own look","u":"waypoint.html#own-look","x":"Style for this waypoint gives one waypoint its own marker look, colours and sizes. Leave it empty to use the HUD's style."},{"p":"The Waypoint component","h":"Events","u":"waypoint.html#events","x":"On Reached and On Left run when the player walks into and out of the reach radius: open a door, play a sound, show a message. No code needed."},{"p":"The Waypoint component","h":"Removing a marker","u":"waypoint.html#removing-a-marker","x":"Switch the Waypoint (or its object) off, or destroy the object: the marker, arrow, compass icon and blip disappear at once."},{"p":"The Waypoint HUD","h":"The Waypoint HUD","u":"hud.html#the-waypoint-hud","x":"One Waypoint HUD in the scene draws every waypoint for one camera. The Setup Wizard adds it; you can also use GameObject > Bearing > Waypoint HUD ."},{"p":"The Waypoint HUD","h":"Look","u":"hud.html#look","x":"Press a preset button (Explorer, Tactical, Minimal, Holo) and the Game view changes at once. Save as my own style saves the current look as a Style asset you can change in every detail. See Make it yours ."},{"p":"The Waypoint HUD","h":"Show","u":"hud.html#show","x":"Setting What it does Markers on screen An icon above each visible waypoint, with its name and distance. Edge arrows For waypoints off screen or behind the player: an arrow at the screen edge, with the icon and distance. Compass bar Directions and waypoint icons along the top (or bottom). Radar A round or square radar in a corner, with an optional map picture. Units Metres, feet, or plain numbers."},{"p":"The Waypoint HUD","h":"Player and camera","u":"hud.html#player-and-camera","x":"Setting What it does View Camera The camera players see through. Empty = the camera tagged MainCamera (looked up every frame, so camera switches just work). Player Distances, reaching and the radar centre are measured from here. Empty = the object tagged Player (found by itself), else the camera. The inspector says which. Plane XZ for 3D games, XY for 2D games ( 2D games )."},{"p":"The Waypoint HUD","h":"Full-screen map","u":"hud.html#full-screen-map","x":"Setting What it does Open with key M by default (None = only from code or a button: ToggleMap() ). Free the mouse while open Unlocks the mouse while the map is open and puts it back after. Pause the game while open Sets Time.timeScale to 0 while the map is open."},{"p":"The Waypoint HUD","h":"More","u":"hud.html#more","x":"Setting What it does Keep clear of my own HUD Screen areas (fractions of the screen from its top-left: x, y, width, height) that edge arrows keep out of, for your own health bar or quest list. Shown as orange boxes in the Game view while not playing. Hide When Paused Hide the HUD while Time.timeScale is 0. Only these groups Show only waypoints in these groups (empty = all); for split screen. Fade behind walls on layers Markers behind these layers fade. Nothing = off. At most 16 wall checks a frame, spread over waypoints. World north rotation Which world direction is north, in degrees. UI Scale"},{"p":"The Waypoint HUD","h":"Beacons","u":"hud.html#beacons","x":"A beacon is a pillar of light rising from a place, so the player sees where to go from far away. The HUD draws it, so it looks the same in Built-in, URP and HDRP with nothing to set up. Turn it on in the style's Beacons section and pick which places get one (the objective, the objective and the tracked place, or every place the player knows); each place can say Always or Never (Show on > Beacon). At most the nearest 6 are drawn at once (Max Beacons; 0 = no limit), so the sky never fills with pillars. Height, width, opacity, colour, a slow glow and the fade as the player arrives are all setting"},{"p":"The Waypoint HUD","h":"Where each part sits","u":"hud.html#where-each-part-sits","x":"Every look starts from the same layout, on one 28 px margin (at 1080p) from the screen edges: Part Default place Compass bar top centre, 28 px from the top Objective tracker top right, its top on the compass bar's line, 28 px from the right Minimap bottom left, 28 px from the left and the bottom Discovery banner centred under the compass bar Edge arrows along the screen edges, kept clear of the minimap and the tracker On a narrow screen the tracker keeps its corner and narrows (long names end in \"...\"). Only when there is no room beside the compass at all (for example one half of a side-by-sid"},{"p":"The Waypoint HUD","h":"Layout: nudge, size and opacity per part","u":"hud.html#layout-nudge-size-and-opacity-per-part","x":"The style's Layout section moves the compass, minimap, tracker and banner a little from where their own settings put them, sizes each about its middle and makes each see-through. Offsets are in pixels at 1080p, so they look the same at every resolution. Use it to make room for your own HUD."},{"p":"The Waypoint HUD","h":"Language","u":"hud.html#language","x":"Set a Text Table on the HUD (More > Language) to show every word in your game's language. See Code and your own UI ."},{"p":"The Waypoint HUD","h":"How it behaves","u":"hud.html#how-it-behaves","x":"Crowds: each style sets the most markers, arrows, compass icons and radar blips at once. The objective wins, then higher Priority, then the nearest. Reaching is decided once a frame from the nearest player, however many HUDs there are. Hiding everything: SetVisible(false) for cutscenes and menus. Edge arrows never overlap each other, keep clear of the compass bar and the radar, and stay inside the screen's safe area. Behind the player an arrow points down or to the side, never up, so it reads as \"turn around\". Split screen: one HUD per camera; each draws in its camera's part of the screen. Per"},{"p":"Make it yours: presets and styles","h":"Make it yours: presets and styles","u":"make-it-yours.html#make-it-yours-presets-and-styles","x":"Everything players see can be changed or switched off, without code."},{"p":"Make it yours: presets and styles","h":"Presets","u":"make-it-yours.html#presets","x":"Preset Look Explorer Ivory icons on dark pins, a gold objective with a beacon, a warm compass and a gold-rimmed minimap. Fits most games. Tactical Corner brackets, capitals, amber objective, degree numbers, a square radar. Minimal Icons and numbers only: no names, no compass bar, no radar. Holo Science fiction: cyan rings on dark glass, degree numbers, an amber objective and a cyan scan. Switch while playing with WaypointHud.UsePreset or NextPreset (the demos do it with Tab)."},{"p":"Make it yours: presets and styles","h":"Compass and minimap frames","u":"make-it-yours.html#compass-and-minimap-frames","x":"Any look can wear any frame (the look gives the colours, the frame the shape): Setting Choices Compass > Skin Bar (rounded, the default), Open Rail (one fine line with fading ends, no bar: the quietest), Fantasy (short pointed ends, a fine inner rim, a stud at each end), Sci-fi (cut corners, corner brackets, a short lit segment) Compass > Curved Bends the compass gently with the view (Curve Depth, about 10 px); letters and icons stay upright Radar > Frame Skin Plain , Fantasy (a second rim, notches, a diamond at N, E, S and W), Sci-fi (four arcs, degree marks; brackets on a square map) The fra"},{"p":"Make it yours: presets and styles","h":"Your own style","u":"make-it-yours.html#your-own-style","x":"On the HUD, press Save as my own style : it saves the current look as a Style asset in Assets/RareGeometryData/Bearing/Styles and uses it. Or Assets > Create > Bearing > Style . Changes show at once in the Game view. Section You can change Colours Marker colour, objective colour, overall size, fade time. Text Your own font, sizes, colour, capitals, bold, shadow, outline. Markers on screen On or off, icon size, background (none, circle, ring, square, diamond, pin, brackets), colours, when names show (off, always, when looked at, when near), distance, shrinking and fading with distance, the obje"},{"p":"Make it yours: presets and styles","h":"Per waypoint","u":"make-it-yours.html#per-waypoint","x":"Each Waypoint can use its own colour, its own icon sprite and its own Style (Own look), and switch any part on or off."},{"p":"Make it yours: presets and styles","h":"Your own pictures","u":"make-it-yours.html#your-own-pictures","x":"Icons: pick Custom on a Waypoint and drag in any sprite. Marker background: set the style's Background to Custom and pick a Background Image. Edge arrow: set Arrow to Custom and pick an Arrow Image (pointing right). Radar player: pick a Player Image (pointing up). White pictures on transparent work best: they take each waypoint's colour."},{"p":"Make it yours: presets and styles","h":"More to fine-tune","u":"make-it-yours.html#more-to-fine-tune","x":"Tracker: Display shows icon, name and distance (Full), icon and name (Short), or icon and distance (Icon). Banner: fade in and out times and a slide in from above (off with reduced motion). Full-screen map: opening animation time, region name colour, the legend panels' colour. Own icons: Keep its own colours shows a full-colour picture as drawn instead of tinting it. Layout: nudge, size and fade the compass, minimap, tracker and banner (see the HUD )."},{"p":"Make it yours: presets and styles","h":"Your own UI","u":"make-it-yours.html#your-own-ui","x":"Turn off any part on the HUD and draw it yourself from what the HUD worked out. See Code and your own UI ."},{"p":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","h":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","u":"map.html#maps-minimap-and-full-screen-map","x":"A Waypoint Map is a picture of your level from above. The radar shows it as a minimap , and M opens it as a full-screen map with the player and every waypoint on it. Waypoints and the player move live on the map; the picture itself is made once, in the editor (it is not a live camera view)."},{"p":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","h":"Add one","u":"map.html#add-one","x":"Setup Wizard step 3: Add Map And Make It , or GameObject > Bearing > Waypoint Map , then Fit To Scene and Make Clean Map . The blue box in the Scene view is the area the map covers. Its top edge is north (the world's forward, Z)."},{"p":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","h":"Three kinds of picture","u":"map.html#three-kinds-of-picture","x":"Picture from What it is Best for Clean map A clean, flat map drawn from your level's shapes: roads, paths, grass, sand, water, rock, buildings and forest, with relief shading and outlines. No camera, so it is the same in Built-in, URP and HDRP. Most 3D games Photo from above A picture taken by a camera looking straight down. 2D games, or levels the Clean map can't read My own picture Any top-down picture you painted, north at the top. Hand-made treasure maps, stylised games"},{"p":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","h":"Making a Clean map","u":"map.html#making-a-clean-map","x":"Press Preview . The map is drawn in the inspector without saving anything. Anything shown in pink wasn't recognised. It's listed under the preview: pick what each one is (Road, Building, Water, Leave off the map...) and press Use . A rule is added and the preview is drawn again. Press Make Clean Map . The picture is saved in Assets/RareGeometryData/Bearing/Maps . How it decides what things are, first match wins: A Waypoint Map Category component on the object or a parent (add it to anything to say exactly what it is). Your rules (Colours, rules and finishing): words in a material name, object "},{"p":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","h":"The full-screen map","u":"map.html#the-full-screen-map","x":"M (or hud.ToggleMap() ) opens it. Drag to move, wheel or + / - to zoom, Me or Space to centre on the player, M or Escape to close. It shows the player arrow, every waypoint with its name, discovered region names, fog of war and a legend for Clean maps. On the HUD you choose the key, whether the mouse is freed (default) and whether the game pauses while it's open. Its look (colours, sizes, words) is in the style's Full-screen map section. Coordinate grid. Full-screen map > Grid draws columns A, B, C... and rows 1, 2, 3... over the map (square cells; Grid Columns sets how many across, Grid Color"},{"p":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","h":"Search","u":"map.html#search","x":"Players press F (or the Search button on the controls bar) and type part of a place's name or its category. Up and Down choose, Enter goes: the map zooms in enough for the place to show, turns to its floor, centres on it and opens its card. Only places the player knows are found, never an undiscovered place's name. The key and an off switch are on the HUD (Full-screen map); the words are in the style's map controls. From code: hud.SearchMap(\"mill\") , hud.MapSearchResults , hud.PickSearchResult() ."},{"p":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","h":"Zoom detail","u":"map.html#zoom-detail","x":"Zoomed out, small places (chests, collectibles) can wait for the player to zoom in: the style's Full-screen map > Zoom Detail sets from which zoom each category shows. The objective, tracked places and the player's own marks always show. The Chart Table's Map tab shows what appears zoomed out, half way and zoomed in."},{"p":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","h":"Why not a live camera map?","u":"map.html#why-not-a-live-camera-map","x":"A live minimap renders the level a second time every frame through an extra camera, which works differently in each render pipeline and costs performance. A made picture looks the same everywhere, costs nothing, and never breaks with a Unity or pipeline update. If your level changes a lot while playing, make a few pictures and swap WaypointMap.Picture ."},{"p":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","h":"The map explains itself","u":"map.html#the-map-explains-itself","x":"Controls bar along the bottom: move, zoom, centre, place a marker, fast travel (when a travel point is on the map), legend and close, each with its key, and with gamepad buttons once a gamepad is used (Glyphs: Auto, Keyboard, Gamepad, Both). Click Zoom, Centre, Legend or Close on the bar to do it. Place cards: point at a place (or move the gamepad crosshair onto it) to see its name, state, category and distance, with a Fast travel button when the place allows it. The card sits beside the place, never on it. First-open hint: a line at the top the first time the map opens in a session (or every "},{"p":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","h":"Bridges","u":"map.html#bridges","x":"The Clean map draws bridges, piers, jetties, boardwalks and docks (by object or material name, or a Map Category of Bridge ) over the water and paths they cross, whatever order things are drawn in, with a darker edge. Colour: Clean > Bridge."},{"p":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","h":"Buildings, caves and sections: map areas","u":"map.html#buildings-caves-and-sections-map-areas","x":"A map can be the map of one building, cave, district or dungeon section. Add a Waypoint Map to it, size its box to the building and tick Is Area (Waypoint Map > Area and floors). While the player is inside the box (and the Area Height range), the minimap and the full-screen map show the area's map; walking out goes back to the map around it. Setting What it does Area Name The name on the maps (the object's name when empty). Area Height The heights the area covers (both 0 = any height): a cellar under a house is its own area. Priority Where areas overlap, the higher priority wins (a room in a b"},{"p":"Maps: minimap and full-screen map","h":"Floors","u":"map.html#floors","x":"Fill Floors with a name and the heights of each floor. Bake All Floors draws each floor from its own heights only, so the roof and the floors above are cut away, and sets each floor's picture (Clean maps and photos both). The player's floor follows their height, with Floor Hysteresis metres to spare, so stairs and jumps never make it Put the boundary between two floors about halfway up the stairs, not just under the upper floor: the player's height is measured at their feet, and a player standing on the upper floor must be more than Floor Hysteresis above the boundary to count as upstairs. fli"},{"p":"Fog of war and region discovery","h":"Fog of war and region discovery","u":"discovery.html#fog-of-war-and-region-discovery","x":""},{"p":"Fog of war and region discovery","h":"Fog of war","u":"discovery.html#fog-of-war","x":"Tick Fog of war on the Waypoint Map. The map starts covered and is uncovered round the player as they explore, on the minimap and the full-screen map. Setting What it does Reveal radius How far round the player the fog lifts, in metres. Fog cell size The fog's grid, in metres (smaller is finer; the default 3 m makes a 300 m map 100 x 100 cells). Fog colour Its alpha is how much of the land still shows through (0.94 = only a faint shape). Hide waypoints in fog Waypoints under the fog are left off the map (the current objective always shows). From code: map.Fog.RevealAll() , RevealCircle , Revea"},{"p":"Fog of war and region discovery","h":"Regions","u":"discovery.html#regions","x":"A Waypoint Map Region is a named area (a village, a forest, a district). Add one with GameObject > Bearing > Waypoint Map Region or the map's Add Region button, then size its box, or give it its own shape: Draw it: set Shape to Polygon, press Edit outline in its inspector, then in the Scene view Shift+click on the ground to add a corner, drag the dots to move them and Ctrl+click a dot to remove it. Corners sit on the ground. Fill it from the map: put the region's object on the area in the map picture (a field, a lake, a district on the Clean map) and press Fill from map . It floods out over si"},{"p":"Fog of war and region discovery","h":"Towers, maps and story reveals","u":"discovery.html#towers-maps-and-story-reveals","x":"A Waypoint Region Reveal uncovers part of the map at once. Make one with GameObject > Bearing > Region Reveal : Tower (walk up to it): a big circle clears outward from the tower while the map opens, so the player watches it. Item (call Reveal on pickup): a whole region clears. Call Reveal() from your pickup (a UnityEvent works). Event (call Reveal from your story): a circle clears; the places inside stay to be found. The clearing spreads over Reveal Time and the places inside are discovered as it reaches them, each playing its own reveal. Pick which categories it discovers, a sound and an even"},{"p":"Fog of war and region discovery","h":"Region progress","u":"discovery.html#region-progress","x":"The full-screen map writes how many of each discovered region's places the player has found next to its name (\"Old Mill 3/7\"). The words are the style's Full-screen map > Region Progress ( {name} , {found} , {total} ); turn it off with Show Region Progress. From code: region.CountPlaces(out found, out total) ."},{"p":"Fog of war and region discovery","h":"Saving","u":"discovery.html#saving","x":"WaypointSave.Capture() includes the fog and which regions were discovered. Restore puts them back, also for maps and regions in scenes loaded later."},{"p":"Fog of war and region discovery","h":"Reveal animations","u":"discovery.html#reveal-animations","x":"When a place changes state, its icons play a short animation on the screen marker, the compass, the minimap and the full-screen map. By default a discovered place's \"?\" flips over to its real icon and a ring bursts out; becoming the objective pulses; completing pops. Everything is a setting in the style's Reveal animations section: Setting What it does Enabled All reveal animations on or off. Discovered, Activated, Completed, Other Change One animation per kind of change: Kind (None, Pop, Flip, Pulse, Ring, Flip And Ring, Drop, Spin, Wiggle), Duration, Easing (Linear, Ease Out, Ease In Out, Ov"},{"p":"Objective trails, events and saving","h":"Objective trails, events and saving","u":"trails.html#objective-trails-events-and-saving","x":""},{"p":"Objective trails, events and saving","h":"A trail of objectives","u":"trails.html#a-trail-of-objectives","x":"The quickest way is an objective chain in the Chart Table : select the places in order, press New chain , then Wire . By hand: Put a Waypoint on each step. On each one set Reach Radius (for example 6), When Reached to Done: show the next waypoint , and Next to the following step. On the last one pick Done: mark it completed . Set the first one's State to Active (the objective). When the player reaches a step, it becomes Completed (dimmed with a check on the minimap and map) and the next one switches on and becomes the objective. The Showcase and Chart Table demos are built this way."},{"p":"Objective trails, events and saving","h":"Events","u":"trails.html#events","x":"Every Waypoint has On Reached and On Left (UnityEvents in the inspector) and the C# events Reached and Left . Use them to open doors, start dialogue, give rewards or update your quest log."},{"p":"Objective trails, events and saving","h":"Groups","u":"trails.html#groups","x":"Give waypoints a Group (\"Shops\", \"Enemies\") and show or hide them together: WaypointRegistry.SetGroupVisible(\"Shops\", false);"},{"p":"Objective trails, events and saving","h":"Saving","u":"trails.html#saving","x":"WaypointSave remembers which waypoints are on, reached and their state (Undiscovered, Discovered, Active, Completed, Hidden), as a short text for your own save system: string text = WaypointSave.Capture(); // when saving WaypointSave.Restore(text); // after the scene loads Each Waypoint has a Save Id , filled in for you. If you duplicate a waypoint, the scene check offers a new id."},{"p":"2D and top-down games","h":"2D and top-down games","u":"2d.html#2d-and-top-down-games","x":"Set the HUD's Plane to XY . The Setup Wizard does this for you when the camera is a 2D (orthographic, facing forward) camera. Markers and edge arrows work the same as in 3D. The radar matches the screen: up on the radar is up on screen. The player arrow shows where the player faces (its transform's up). The compass bar is usually not wanted in 2D; switch it off on the HUD. A Waypoint Map with Plane XY bakes the level seen from the camera's side. Put markers where you want them with Mark Above Object off and an Offset (for example 1.4 up). The Top-Down 2D demo shows all of this."},{"p":"Why Can't I See It? and the scene check","h":"Why Can't I See It? and the scene check","u":"debugger.html#why-cant-i-see-it-and-the-scene-check","x":""},{"p":"Why Can't I See It? and the scene check","h":"The window","u":"debugger.html#the-window","x":"Tools > Bearing > Why Can't I See It? (Scene Overview) Scene check lists setup problems with a one-click fix where there is one: Problem Fix No Waypoint HUD in the scene Add HUD The HUD has no camera Use the scene's camera A 2D camera with a 3D (XZ) HUD Use 2D (XY) Two HUDs drawing for one camera (select them) Custom icon with no sprite Use the Objective icon Switch To Next with no Next, or Next pointing to itself (select it) Hide Within larger than Show Within, so it never shows Clear Hide Within Two waypoints sharing a save id New id More than one Waypoint Map, or a map with no picture Bake "},{"p":"Why Can't I See It? and the scene check","h":"In the game: the debug overlay","u":"debugger.html#in-the-game-the-debug-overlay","x":"Add Waypoint Debug Overlay to the HUD's object and press F8 while playing for the same list on screen (the key can be changed). It makes text only while it is open. Remove it before shipping if players shouldn't find it."},{"p":"Why Can't I See It? and the scene check","h":"The reasons","u":"debugger.html#the-reasons","x":"Reason Meaning No camera Set the HUD's View Camera, or tag your camera MainCamera. Group hidden WaypointRegistry.SetGroupVisible(group, false) was called. Too close / too far Hide Within or Show Within. Reached When Reached is Hide While Near and the player is inside the reach radius. Markers off / edge arrows off Switched off on the waypoint, the HUD or the style. Screen full More important or nearer waypoints took the places. Raise its Priority or the style's Max Count."},{"p":"Code and your own UI","h":"Code and your own UI","u":"extending.html#code-and-your-own-ui","x":"Nothing here is needed for the main jobs. Everything is in the RareGeometry.Bearing namespace."},{"p":"Code and your own UI","h":"Waypoints from code","u":"extending.html#waypoints-from-code","x":"var w = gameObject.AddComponent<Waypoint>(); w.Label = \"Old Mill\"; w.Icon = WaypointIcon.Flag; w.IsObjective = true; w.Reached += wp => Debug.Log(\"Reached \" + wp.Label); w.Hide(); // switch off (markers disappear) w.Show(); // switch on A waypoint with no object of yours (a quest coordinate): var drop = Waypoint.CreateAt(new Vector3(120f, 0f, 40f), \"Drop Zone\", WaypointIcon.Pin); drop.Position = newPosition; drop.SetAsCurrentObjective(); // the only objective now drop.Remove(); // gone Useful: WaypointRegistry.Waypoints , WaypointRegistry.CurrentObjective , WaypointRegistry.FindById(id) , Wayp"},{"p":"Code and your own UI","h":"The HUD from code","u":"extending.html#the-hud-from-code","x":"var hud = WaypointHud.Main; hud.UsePreset(WaypointPreset.Tactical); hud.ShowRadar = false; hud.Units = WaypointUnits.Feet; hud.RadarZoom = 2f; // closer hud.SetCamera(vehicleCamera); // camera switches (null = Main Camera) hud.SetPlayer(newCharacter); // null = the object tagged Player hud.SetVisible(false); // cutscenes and menus float ms = hud.LastDrawMilliseconds; // the HUD's own cost last frame hud.Style.Marker.iconSize = 40f; hud.Style.Touch(); // after changing style fields from code"},{"p":"Code and your own UI","h":"Your own UI","u":"extending.html#your-own-ui","x":"Each frame the HUD works out, for every waypoint: distance, height, whether it's on screen, its screen position and edge angle, its compass bearing and its radar point. Read it after the Drawn event: hud.Drawn += h => { if (h.TryGetView(myWaypoint, out var v) && !v.IsOnScreen) myArrow.SetPositionAndAngle(v.ScreenPosition, v.ScreenAngle); }; Switch off the parts you replace ( ShowMarkers , ShowEdgeArrows , ShowCompass , ShowRadar ) and keep the rest. WaypointView.Reason says why a waypoint isn't drawn."},{"p":"Code and your own UI","h":"Your own part, drawn every frame","u":"extending.html#your-own-part-drawn-every-frame","x":"Turn Bearing's part off (the HUD's Show switches), then add your own display: sealed class MyCompass : IWaypointDisplay { public void Draw(WaypointHud hud, IReadOnlyList<WaypointView> views) { foreach (var v in views) if (v.CompassShown || v.Waypoint.IsObjective) PlaceIcon(v.Waypoint, v.Bearing); } } hud.ShowCompass = false; hud.AddDisplay(new MyCompass()); Each view has the screen position and angle (markers, edge arrows), Bearing (a compass), RadarPoint (a minimap), Distance, Height and what Bearing itself showed. The list is for this frame only."},{"p":"Code and your own UI","h":"Words for other languages","u":"extending.html#words-for-other-languages","x":"Every word Bearing shows (the style's words, the controls bar, the place card, the player options, place, region and area names) goes through one place, WaypointText : Text Table (Create > Bearing > Text Table): press Collect Words to list every word (from your styles, the places in the open scenes, and the words shown while you played), write yours next to each, then set it on the HUD (More > Language) or with WaypointText.Table = table; . Unity Localization: add Waypoint Localization (it appears when the Localization package is installed) and pick a String Table whose keys are Bearing's Engl"},{"p":"Code and your own UI","h":"Your own banners","u":"extending.html#your-own-banners","x":"hud.ShowBanner(\"Bridge repaired\", \"Chapter 2\"); shows your own banner in the style's banner look, queued behind any showing now. WaypointSaveKeys uses it to say \"Saved\" and \"Loaded\"."},{"p":"Code and your own UI","h":"Your own input","u":"extending.html#your-own-input","x":"Bearing reads only the map key (M), the debug overlay key (F8) and, with the Input System package, the gamepad on the open map. To drive it from your own input code (Rewired, your own actions), set the two hooks in BearingInput : BearingInput.KeyDownOverride = key => myInput.WasPressed(key); BearingInput.PadOverride = () => new WaypointPadState { Move = myInput.MapMove, Place = myInput.Confirm }; Or turn the keys off (Map key None on the HUD) and call hud.ToggleMap() , ZoomMap and PanMap yourself."},{"p":"Code and your own UI","h":"Saving","u":"extending.html#saving","x":"WaypointSave.Capture() and WaypointSave.Restore(text) ; see Objective trails, events and saving . Or let Bearing save itself: RareGeometry.Core.SaveService.Save(\"slot1\") and Load(\"slot1\") store every waypoint, fog, region and the player's pin under the key bearing.waypoints (one JSON file per slot, or your save product when one is installed). Every waypoint and region carries a StableId , so the same object is found again."},{"p":"Code and your own UI","h":"Explaining","u":"extending.html#explaining","x":"WaypointDiagnostics.Explain(waypoint) returns the same plain sentence as the debugger."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Upgrading","u":"upgrading.html#upgrading","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"1.0.0","u":"upgrading.html#100","x":"The first release. Nothing to do."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"How updates work","u":"upgrading.html#how-updates-work","x":"Your own styles, maps and other data live in Assets/RareGeometryData/Bearing , which updates never touch. Public names, menu items and saved fields are kept between versions; anything that changes is listed here with what to do. To update: import the new version over the old one. You can move or rename Bearing's own folder ( Assets/RareGeometry/Bearing ); it still works. Keep the shared Core and EditorUI folders where they are."},{"p":"Questions people ask","h":"Questions people ask","u":"faq.html#questions-people-ask","x":"Does it need code? No. Everything is set up in the inspector, the Setup Wizard and the Chart Table. Code is there when you want it (see Code and your own UI ). Which Unity versions and render pipelines? Unity 6 (6000.0 and newer), Built-in, URP and HDRP. The HUD is drawn with UI Toolkit, so it looks the same in every pipeline; nothing needs a shader of yours. Does it work with my character controller and camera? Yes. The HUD only needs a camera to draw for and an object to measure distances from. Pick them in the Setup Wizard; neither needs a tag. A camera your game makes while playing is foun"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting","x":"First open Why Can't I See It? (Tools > Bearing). It explains most problems and fixes many in one click. Problem Answer Nothing shows at all The scene needs a Waypoint HUD with a camera. The scene check adds one. Nothing shows in the Game view before Play Turn on Preview In Edit Mode on the HUD. A marker shows twice Two HUDs draw for the same camera; the scene check finds them. A marker disappears when I get close Hide Within is set, or When Reached is Hide While Near. A marker shows in the wrong place Turn Mark Above Object off and use Offset, or call RefreshTop() after the model changes size"},{"p":"Support","h":"Support","u":"support.html#support","x":"Need help? Every message gets an answer. In Unity, Tools > Bearing > Help and Support lists every support address (Discord, bug reports, email) with Copy buttons, and Copy system info copies the Bearing version, Unity version, render pipeline and platform. Paste it in your message. Discord: https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu (ask in #help) Report a bug: https://github.com/raregeometry/Support/issues Email: raregeometrysupport@gmail.com Website: https://raregeometry.github.io/bearing/ (with the playable demo at https://raregeometry.github.io/bearing/demo/) Enjoying Bearing? A short review on the Ass"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changelog","u":"changelog.html#changelog","x":"All notable changes to Bearing. Format: Keep a Changelog. Versions: Semantic Versioning."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[1.0.0] - Unreleased","u":"changelog.html#100---unreleased","x":""},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changed","u":"changelog.html#changed","x":"Help and Support shows the website (https://raregeometry.github.io/bearing/) and the bug tracker; the online manual link appears once the manual is on the website. Shared Core synced to 8d41ac2 (studio website, bug tracker and support email live). Shared Core synced to 5f55d5d (test-only: registry tests no longer depend on test order). Clean map floor pictures (a height band) are drawn flat, without relief shading, so floors read cleanly round furniture and walls. Calm defaults: markers on screen within 40 m, edge arrows for the objective, the compass within 150 m; caps 8 markers, 4 edge arrow"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added","x":"The minimap never ends in a hard line or shows the world past the map: its frame is solid wherever a map is shown, and past the map picture it carries on with the map's own edge (the sea round an island), smoothed, out past the frame's diagonal so a turning minimap is covered at every angle. Edge Mode on the Waypoint Map sets it for the minimap and the full map: Continue Edge Colour (default), Stop At Edge (the minimap's view stays inside the map and the arrow moves towards the edge instead) or Themed Surround (the look's frame colour). Range rings and the centre cross step back over a map pic"}];
